3.3.3. Applications
It should come as no surprise that if we restrict the operations allowed on a list, those operations can be performed very
quickly. The big surprise, however, is that the small number of operations left are so powerful and important. We give
three of the many applications of stacks. The third application gives a deep insight into how programs are organized.
Balancing Symbols
Compilers check your programs for syntax errors, but frequently a lack of one symbol (such as a missing brace or
comment starter) will cause the compiler to spill out a hundred lines of diagnostics without identifying the real error.
A useful tool in this situation is a program that checks whether everything is balanced. Thus, every right brace,
bracket, and parenthesis must correspond to their left counterparts. The sequence [()] is legal, but [(]) is wrong.
