7.6.1. Analysis of Mergesort

Mergesort is a classic example of the techniques used to analyze recursive routines. It is not obvious that mergesort can
easily be rewritten without recursion (it can), so we have to write a recurrence relation for the running time. We will
assume that n is a power of 2, so that we always split into even halves. For n = 1, the time to mergesort is constant,
which we will denote by 1. Otherwise, the time to mergesort n numbers is equal to the time to do two recursive
mergesorts of size n/2, plus the time to merge, which is linear. The equations below say this exactly:
T(1) = 1
T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n
