What is a dictionary (map) in C++?
In C++ STL, a map is a container that stores data in key–value pairs.
Each key is unique and maps to exactly one value.
map<int, string> dict;
dict[1] = "One";
dict[2] = "Two";
Here 1 → "One" and 2 → "Two" are key–value pairs.
Why use a map (dictionary) for Sparse Matrices?

If we store the full matrix as a 2D array, we waste memory on storing zeros.

A map can efficiently store only the non-zero elements by using the position (row, column) as the key and the value as the data
13
Row 	 Col  	Value
   0    	  2    	   5
   2    	  0            3
   3    	  3            8
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
using namespace std;

int main() 
{
    // key = (row, col), value = matrix element
    map<pair<int, int>, int> sparseMatrix;

    // inserting non-zero values
    sparseMatrix[{0,2}] = 5;
    sparseMatrix[{2,0}] = 3;
    sparseMatrix[{3,3}] = 8;

    // accessing values
    cout << "Value at (0,2): " << sparseMatrix[{0,2}] << endl;
    cout << "Value at (2,0): " << sparseMatrix[{2,0}] << endl;
}
Advantages of using map in Sparse Matrices

Memory efficient → only stores non-zero values.
Fast access → direct access using (row, col) as the key.
Flexible size → no need to fix matrix dimensions.
